in 2003 nobel laureate vitaly ginsberg  said that there are a number of holy  grails left to be discovered in physics  that  if found would revolutionize our  understanding of the world we live in  now his list included all kinds of  exotic phenomena and technologies like  dark matter detection and controlled  nuclear fusion  but unfortunately most of these have  we've yet to find  but 2020 might finally have produced  some good news because  according to a paper that just came out  in nature a team of physicists  just discovered number two on ginsburg's  list  room temperature superconductors now  this  is incredibly exciting because room  temperature superconductors  are a technology that if we can find a  way to mass produce them  will revolutionize energy efficiency and  transform electronics across the board  and i'm here at  the university of rochester where this  discovery has been  found to interview the two scientists  behind it  dr ashken salamat and dr ranga diaz  so stay tuned for an exclusive insight  into this groundbreaking discovery  but first what exactly is a  superconductor and  why is a room temperature one so  important well it all comes down to  energy and heat when we talk about  electricity  what we're really talking about is the  flow of electrons through a material  like a wire from point a  to point b but it's not always that easy  for these electrons to flow because  they're  constantly interacting with atoms within  that wire along the way  and all these interactions create a kind  of internal friction known as electrical  resistance  and kind of like how your hands get hot  when you rub them together due to  friction  this electrical resistance creates a  load of waste heat  too which is why things like phones and  laptops often overheat if you use them  too much  and all this waste heat really adds up  in fact it's estimated that between five  to ten percent of  all the electricity we send down the  grid gets wasted by it  and that adds up to cost of around 20  billion dollars  per year in the us alone now back in  1911  a dutch physicist discovered something  astonishing  he was playing around with the metal  mercury to see how its resistance would  behave  at really cold temperatures he reasoned  that  because resistance is caused by  vibrating atoms  and atoms vibrate because of heat energy  then if you made the metal colder its  resistance should gradually decrease too  down to some finite value  but instead of doing that suddenly at  just 4.2 degrees above absolute zero the  mercury's internal resistance  disappeared entirely for some strange  reason  below that critical temperature the  mercury became a literal  perfect conductor to the point that if  you put a current through it it would  keep flowing forever  and ever like some kind of perpetual  motion machine  he called this bizarre phenomenon  superconductivity and in fact won the  nobel prize for it two years later  and since then we've discovered a number  of different superconducting materials  which we use in  many kinds of cutting-edge tech like mri  machines  particle accelerators even fusion  reactors  we even use them to levitate stuff  thanks to a thing known as the meissner  effect  where a magnetic field is expelled so  strongly outside of the superconductor  it can even levitate trains  so yeah superconductors are pretty much  the coolest thing ever  and therein lies the problem because  unfortunately all the ones we're  currently using  require extremely cold temperatures to  maintain  which means they use a lot of energy in  fact that's one of the reasons why mri  scans cost so much because  they need all this liquid helium which  is a resource we are rapidly running out  of  to keep them cool at four degrees kelvin  just a smidge above absolute zero and  even though tons of headway has been  made in the quest for a room temperature  one  still the best we've achieved so far is  -23 degrees  celsius so still well below freezing and  when you consider that still today  over 60 percent of our electricity comes  from climate change causing fossil fuels  you can see why this is a holy grail of  physics  so on that note let's meet the two  physicists who have finally found one  can you tell me what it is exactly that  you've found  we have engineered a new material which  we call it carbonaceous sulfur hydride  it has carbon and a sulfur and hydrogen  it's a room temperature superconductor  which never existed on earth before  the superconductor at 15 degrees celsius  so explain to me  what it is that you guys have done  differently our scientific speciality  is actually exploring materials under  very high pressure conditions  we use something called a diamond anvil  cell this is a handheld device  that utilizes the optical mechanical  properties of diamond  and really what it is is a search engine  for exploring compositional face space  we can throw a bunch of materials into  our little handheld device  and we can modulate pressure as well as  temperature  and it allows us to look for materials  that mother nature herself isn't very  good at making or in fact can't even  make  and in fact what we've been able to do  is take organically derived materials  so very common abundant materials from  earth  and pressurize these in such a manner to  allow them to enter these exotic states  this is an unexpected system the  theorists in our community have all been  looking at very specific types of  systems  and this has come from out of nowhere  this is really the genius of my  colleague ranga diaz  in being able to not be  blinded by the bias in the community and  really have a fresh set of eyes and  looking at face space  below a certain critical temperature for  certain materials  electrons can actually start to see each  other not for physical space but for  energy space  and in fact electrons can start to begin  to pair with each other  over very very large distances but  they're actually coupled  in energy space and this coupling occurs  through motions periodic motions inside  the lattice  and it's this pair of electrons that you  form that now allow you to  demonstrate these beautiful and bizarre  quantum phenomena  the one way you could think about this  is that you have a pair of electrons  and the front one is traveling and takes  all the  all the hits and the back one gets to  sit  and basically have a completely  unaltered path  what we've really tried to do is take a  pure hydrogen framework  and exchange some of the hydrogens for  other larger elements  and as a way exert what we call a  chemical pressure on the system  this allows you to have a lower  mechanical pressure and really the aim  is to try and replace the mechanical  pressure completely with just the  chemical pressure  think of this way so if you have in a  room with 10  skinny people and you feel you know  there's an empty space and you're not  that squeeze  but you're going to replace these 10  people with the uh like in football  defensive  lineman like 300 pounds and now all of a  sudden you're getting squeezed  so instead of mechanically you're  pressing it you can actually chemically  do the same compression that has the  same uh properties  i can think of three different  directions that we can go if you can  really make a bigger quantity  at ambient pressure and ambient  temperature  if you take you know the electricity you  know transmission of electricity  from east coast to the west coast you  know you have a transmission line right  you have to send the current from one  place to another place  you have you need this transmission line  and the two days  when we use this transmission line we  lose about like 200  million megawatt per hour amount of  energy  if i convert that to in terms of dollars  it's  20 billion dollars so imagine you can  replace all these  lines superconduct the transmission line  with the superconducting wires  now that there is no you can transfer  electricity without any loss  one can envision in the future having  solar farms in the southwest of the  united states  and using a superconducting grid to send  energy to the northeast without any loss  or dissipation of energy  and then of course the the  transportation is the the next uh  big improvement that we can have with  this room private superconductor the  superconduct is not just a perfect  conductor  it has this property called magnetic  levitation so you can probably levitate  the  the trains and then you can have a  frictionless  tracks that you can travel faster than  what we are traveling uh now and then  that will  going to change the whole uh  transportation system  so the consumer electronics the  electronics that we use in right  nowadays is semiconducting materials  most of the you know your phone your  laptop you're holding now your cell  phone  and all these computers that we are  talking about the semiconductor world  but imagine you're going to build a  superconducting world  now everything is you have a  superconducting wire superconducting  circuits not just that then nowadays the  the biggest  you know the buzzword is quantum  computing quantum material right  so imagine that this will definitely  help us to even to have a room  temperatures and superconducting qubits  which you know directly going to apply  to this  quantum computing so this has really the  potential to  revolutionize the the whole world if you  can really make  one at ambient conditions  i think this kind of discoveries if i  can really excite young scientists young  students  who are coming to the lab and trying to  uh you know explore their curiosity  if this helped i would say that even  though we're going through this  pandemic you know this dark year of 2020  probably this will help them to really  motivate themselves not to give up  there are a lot more you can do than  what you can find and there's always  hope in this world yes there is rango  and thanks so much to you and ashkan and  your universities for making this  incredible discovery and for letting me  film it i found this  so inspirational and i hope you guys at  home did too  if you did make sure you press that like  button and share this video so that  others get to learn about it too  and please subscribe to my channel so  that you can catch the next one i'll see  you next time
 

